      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "To assess a point-of-care urine trypsinogen-2 (UT) test for the diagnosis of acute pancreatitis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "This was a prospective study of patients presenting to the emergency department with abdominal pain suggestive of acute pancreatitis. A 3-minute point-of-care UT test (Actim Pancreatitis; Medix Biochemica, Kauniainen, Finland) was compared with final diagnosis of acute pancreatitis, which was based on suggestive clinical features, serum lipase and/or amylase levels and imaging."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Of 124 patients included in this study, 69 patients had final diagnosis of acute pancreatitis. The sensitivity and specificity of UT were, respectively, 73.9% (95% CI 61.9% to 83.8%) and 94.6% (95% CI 84.9% to 98.9%)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "The point-of-care UT test for acute pancreatitis had good sensitivity and specificity, and can be used reliably at the bedside to make a positive diagnosis."
        }
      ]},
